    {
      "nasa_id": "19960008387",
      "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
      "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
      "pdf_page_count": 6,
      "pages_processed": 6,
      "passage_count": 170,
      "scientific_findings_verified": false,
      "passages": [
        {
          "text": "N96- 15553\n                                    Solid  Surface  Combustion   Experiment\n                    Flame  Spread  in a Quiescent,   Microgravity   Environment\n                            Implications    of Spread  Rate and Flame  Structure\n\n                      Matthew   Bundy,    Jeff West,  Peter  C.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Thomas,   and  Subrata   Bhattacharjee\n                                         Department    of Mechanical    Engineering\n                                San  Diego  State  University,   San Diego,  CA 92182-0191\n\n                                            Lin Tang  and  Robert  A.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Altenkirch\n                                      Department    of Mechanical    Engineering    and\n                    NSF  Engineering    Research   Center  for Computational     Field  Simulation\n                                      Mississippi    State  University,    Mississippi     State, MS  39762\n                                                                and\n                                                    Kurt Sacksteder\n                                   NASA   Lewis  Research   Center\n                                                         Cleveland,  OH 44135\nIntroduction:\n\nA unique   environment     in which  flame  spreading,    a phenomenon     of fundamental,      scientific    interest,   has\nimportance    to  fire  safety   is that  of spacecraft    in which   the  gravitational     acceleration      is low  compared   with\nthat  of  the  Earth,   i.e., microgravity.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Experiments    aboard  eight  Space  Shuttle   missions   between   October   1990\nand  February   1995  were  conducted    using   the  Solid  Surface  Combustion    Experiment    (SSCE)  payload\napparatus    in an  effort  to determine    the mechanisms     of gas-phase    flame  spread   over  solid  fuel  surfaces    in the\nabsence   of any  buoyancy    induced    or externally   imposed    oxidizer   flow.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The  overall  SSCE  effort  began   in\nDecember    of 1984.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The  SSCE  apparatus    consists   of a sealed   container,   approximately     0.039 m 3, that   is filled  with  a specified\n0 2  / N 2 mixture   at a prescribed    pressure.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Five  of the  experiments    used  a thin  cellulosic    fuel,  ashless    filter\npaper,  3 cm  wide  by  10 cm  long,  0.00825  cm half-thickness,     ignited   in five  different   ambient    conditions.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Three  of  the  experiments,     the  most  recent,  used  thick  polymethylmethacrylate      (PMMA)   samples   0.635 cm\nwide  by  2 cm  long,  0.32 cm half-thickness.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Three  experiments,   STS 41, 40 and  43, were  designed    to evaluate\nthe  effect   of ambient    pressure   on flame  spread   over  the  thin  cellulosic    fuel  while   flights   STS 50 and  47 were\nat the same  pressure    as two  of the  earlier   flights  but  at a lower  oxygen   concentration     in order  to  evaluate    the\neffect   of ambient    oxygen    level  on  the  flame  spread   process   at microgravity.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "For  the PMMA    flights,   two\nexperiments,    STS 54 and  63, were  at the same  pressure   but  different   oxygen   concentrations     while  STS 64 was\nat the  same  oxygen   concentration    as STS 63 but  at a higher   pressure.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Two  orthogonal    views   of the  experiments    were  recorded   on 16 mm  cine-cameras    operating     at 24 frames/s.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "In addition    to filmed   images   of the  side  view   of the  flames  and  surface  view   of the  burning   samples,    solid-\nand  gas-phase    temperatures    were  recorded   using  thermocouples.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The experiment      is battery  powered   and\nfollows   an automated    sequence   upon   activation   by  the Shuttle  Crew.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "In  this  study  we  separate    the SSCE  data  into  two  groups   according    to the  fuel  type:   i) thin  cellulose,   and   ii) thick\nPMMA.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The  experimental    spread   rates  are  compared   with  prediction   from  a number   of models    in an  effort  to\nuncover    the  important     physics   that  characterize    microgravity    flame  spread.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Both  steady   and  unsteady    solutions\n are employed     to explore   the  flame   evolution,    especially    for  thick  fuels.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally,   the  flame   structure    in downward\n spread    is compared    with  the  microgravity    flame   structure    and  modeling     results   to  delineate    the  difference    between\n the  two  configurations     and  the  influence    of normal   gravity.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 1,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
