          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Superposed  on this figure  is the prediction  from  the de Ris theory   [3], V f,&Ris, the prediction  from  a non-radiative\n computational  model  similar  to that  of Frey and  T'ien  [4],  Vf.,,on_,aa, and,  the prediction  from a radiative  model   [5].",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The  non-radiative  models  over-predict   Vf  by almost a factor of 2 to 3.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "Moreover,  the dear  trend seen  in the\n experiments,   an increase  in  V/  with  P**,is completely  missed by these  models.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The model  with  radiation\n produces   the best agreement   (within  10%) with experiment.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The near-perfect   agreement   between   the de Ris formula  and  the  non-radiative  model  needs  some  explanation.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The\n spread  rate is computed  using a succession   of models  in which  the major assumptions   in the de Ris theory   - zero\n hang-distance   (#2), Oseen  flow  (#3), constant  gas density  (#4), no wall blowing   (#5), infinite-rate   chemistry    (#6),\n constant  vaporization  temperature   (#7), constant  solid  density  (#8), constant  gas conductivity    (#9), and no\n radiation  (#10-#13)  - are removed  one by one, and  the resulting  spread  rates are plotted  in Fig.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "3 for two ambient\n pressures  and two  Vg's.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
