          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "On the very  left of the  abscissa  is the  prediction  from  the de Ris formula  (#1), and on the\n extreme  right  are the data (#14) from  the SSCE and  literature   [6] with  the  intermediate    points  being  the various\n mathematical  models  with  increasing   complexity.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "It is clear from this figure  that many  of the assumptions   in the\n de Ris theory  have a canceling   affect, and  that  is why  the non-radiative   model  (#9) produces  the same  spread  rate\n as the de Ris formula  (#1).",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 2,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
