        },
        {
          "text": "Conclusions:\n\nExperimentalresultsfromthe SSCE and  computational       results  from   steady   and  unsteady   modeling    are  presented.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 4,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "It is established    that  for thin  cellulosic    fuel  the  flame  spread  phenomenon       is  fairly  steady   after  the   initial   ignition\n transients    are  over.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 4,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The flame   size  and  the  hang  distance   are much   larger  in microgravity     than   in downward     flame\npropagation.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 4,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "The  fundamental   mechanism    behind   the  striking   differences   between    the  0-g  and   1-g  flame  seems   to\nbe  the  enhancement     of radiation    heat  transfer    in the  quiescent,    microgravity     environment     because    of  the  large\n residence    time.",
          "source_field": "pdf_page_text",
          "pdf_page_number": 4,
          "source_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387",
          "matched_terms": [],
          "evidence_type": "extracted_source_text",
          "research_status": "unclassified",
          "classification_basis": "No recognized page heading found.",
          "classification_status": "requires_manual_review",
          "experimental_result_status": "not_established",
          "scientific_findings_verified": false,
          "nasa_id": "19960008387",
          "title": "Solid surface combustion experiment flame spread in a quiescent, microgravity environment implications of spread rate and flame structure",
          "citation_url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960008387"
        },
        {
          "text": "For thick  fuels,  a premixed   flame  propagation    during   ignition   has been  captured   by  the  unsteady\nmodel   as weU  as the experiments.",
